\description{
Mathematics gains of 1,190 first graders in 312 classrooms and 107
schools, with characteristics of the students, their first-grade teachers
and their schools. The data come from the Study of Instructional
Improvement (SII), an observational study analyzed by Hill, Rowan and Ball
(2005), and are the classroom data of West, Welch and Galecki's (2006)
book \emph{Linear Mixed Models}. Each row is a student.
}
\details{
\strong{Design.} Students are nested in classrooms (1 to 10 students per
classroom) and classrooms in schools (2 to 31 students per school).
\code{yearstea}, \code{mathknow} and \code{mathprep} describe the classroom's teacher and
are constant within a classroom; \code{housepov} is constant within a school.
Because the study is observational, regression coefficients describe
associations rather than causal effects of teachers or schools.

\strong{Coding.} \code{mathgain} is the spring first-grade score minus the spring
kindergarten score (\code{mathkind}), so it can be negative (39 students).
\code{housepov} is stored as a proportion (0.012 to 0.564), not a percentage.
\code{girl} (1 = girl) is called \code{sex} in the original classroom data.
\code{mathknow} is missing for 27 of the 312 classrooms (109 students); the
other variables are complete. Version 1.0.0 changed no values; it corrects
the v0.2.0 help page, which misdescribed the study.

\strong{Suggested analyses.}
\itemize{
\item one-way ANOVA of \code{mathgain} across schools, \code{factor(schoolid)};
\item simple and multiple regression of \code{mathgain} on \code{mathkind}, \code{ses},
\code{minority} and teacher variables such as \code{mathknow};
\item multilevel structure: compare ordinary least-squares standard errors for
classroom- and school-level predictors with those from a model that
allows for clustering.
}
}
\examples{
head(instruction)

# One-way ANOVA: do mean math gains differ across the 107 schools?
